    "display_name": "New Shoes",
    "long_instructions": "You’re building a multiple linear regression model that predicts how long a new pair of shoes will last (in weeks) based on two variables: the distance you walk each week (in miles) and the price of the shoes (in dollars). You have the following training data:\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n| Distance | Price | Time Until Worn Out |\r\n| - | - | - |\r\n| 5 miles | $30 | 40 weeks |\r\n| 10 miles | $45 | 30 weeks |\r\n| 15 miles | $60 | 24 weeks |\r\n| 20 miles | $80 | 18 weeks |\r\n| 20 miles | $30 | 9 weeks |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**☑️ Do This:**\r\n\r\n1. The first training data example has already been added to the `inputs` list. Add the remaining training data examples to the `inputs` list.\r\n2. The first training data example has already been added to the `outputs` list. Add the remaining training data examples to the `outputs` list.\r\n3. Train the model based on the training data inputs and outputs.\r\n4. Run the code and make some predictions. Check that they seem reasonable based on your training data.\r\n\r\n**💻 Coding Tip**\r\n\r\nTo train a linear regression model with `scikit-learn`, recall that you can use code like:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nmodel.fit(x, y)\r\n```\r\n\r\nwhere `x` represents the inputs and `y` represents the outputs.\r\n",
